<h2 id="why-switch">Why teams look for a Freshdesk alternative</h2>
<p>Freshdesk's paid entry plan is not the only number to check. The standalone product's official pricing page lists Growth at US$19, Pro at US$55 and Enterprise at US$89 per agent/month on annual billing, checked October 28, 2026. The same page lists additional Freddy AI Agent sessions at US$49 per 100 and Freddy AI Copilot at US$29 per agent/month on Pro and Enterprise. See the <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/">official Freshdesk pricing page</a> for the current plan table.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>AI costs are separate from the seat price.</strong> The first 500 AI Agent sessions are included as stated on the pricing page, but additional sessions are charged. Estimate usage before comparing an AI-first competitor.</li>
<li><strong>Some controls sit on higher plans.</strong> Freshdesk lists multilingual help desk, custom dashboards, intelligent routing and multiple SLA policies on Pro. Enterprise adds skill-based routing, sandbox, audit logs and allowed-domain or IP controls.</li>
<li><strong>Channel needs can change the product choice.</strong> Standalone Freshdesk is positioned around email-first service. Freshdesk Omni is a separate product for broader channels and has its own plan scope. Do not compare the two as if they are one plan.</li>
<li><strong>Data and stack fit matter.</strong> A filtered ticket export is not a full account export. CRM-first businesses may prefer support records in their CRM, while stores may need order context. Check the export path and test a sample before cancelling.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are reasons to compare products, not proof that Freshdesk is a poor choice. If your team uses its ticketing, portal and reports well, the cost and risk of migration may be greater than the benefit of changing.</p>

<h2 id="zendesk">1. Zendesk: for structured support workflows</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that need ticket ownership, routing, customer context, analytics and a wider service suite. Zendesk's Support Team plan lists email ticketing, ticket routing, prebuilt analytics dashboards, saved responses, customer context, automations and triggers. Suite plans add broader channel and knowledge capabilities. See the <a href="https://www.zendesk.com/pricing/">official pricing page</a>, checked October 28, 2026.</p>
<p>Zendesk is a direct comparison when Freshdesk's plan boundaries or workflow controls no longer fit. Its Support Team plan is US$19 per agent/month on annual billing; Suite Team is US$55 and Suite Professional is US$115 per agent/month on annual billing. Compare the plan that includes your required channels and reports, not just the entry price.</p>
<p><strong>Where the cost changes:</strong> a wider plan range can mean more decisions about bundles, add-ons and setup. Zendesk is not automatically cheaper than Freshdesk. Read our <a href="/zendesk/review/">Zendesk review</a>, <a href="/zendesk/pricing/">Zendesk pricing guide</a> and the existing <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk comparison</a> before deciding.</p>


<h2 id="zoho-desk">2. Zoho Desk: for smaller budgets and Zoho CRM users</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that want support software alongside Zoho CRM or other Zoho apps. Zoho's official feature page lists ticketing, AI assistance through Zia, omnichannel support, self-service, automation, customization, analytics, integrations and a mobile app. Its plan comparison shows a Free edition for up to three users and paid plans whose prices vary by region. The official pages list the features and free tier.</p>
<p>The Free edition is a useful way for a small team to check its workflow before paying. The paid tiers add features and user capacity, but the official page displayed region-specific currency fields in the public page text. We do not list a guessed US starting price. Check the currency and billing term in the vendor's current pricing page.</p>
<p><strong>What to verify:</strong> Zoho Desk makes the most sense when the wider Zoho environment matters. If your team does not use Zoho CRM, compare the integration and administration work rather than choosing it on the free tier alone.</p>


<h2 id="help-scout">3. Help Scout: for a shared inbox and a smaller support team</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that collaborate on customer conversations and publish help content without needing a large set of service administration controls. Help Scout's pricing page lists a Free plan for up to five users, one inbox and one Docs site. Standard is US$25 per user/month, Plus is US$45 and Pro is US$75 on the annual view.</p>
<p>The product is a fit when your support process is conversation-led. The Standard plan lists multiple inboxes, live chat and SMS, messaging channels, knowledge bases, a basic SLA policy and workflows. Plus adds more workflow and routing controls. Review the plan table for inbox limits and extra charges before moving a team with many support addresses.</p>
<p><strong>Plan limits to check:</strong> the Free plan is deliberately small, and the Pro plan has a minimum team size. Help Scout is not a direct replacement for every routing, security or reporting need in a larger help desk.</p>


<h2 id="intercom">4. Intercom: for product-led and AI-assisted support</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> software companies that want customer messaging, a help center, a shared inbox and an AI agent in one service workflow. Intercom's pricing page showed an Essential offer at US$19 per seat/month for new customers, reduced from a regular US$29 rate, and a US$0.99 charge per Fin outcome when checked October 28, 2026. Treat that offer as time-sensitive and confirm the live terms.</p>
<p>Intercom can suit a product-led team that wants to answer questions in the app and automate common requests. The cost model needs more than a seat calculation: forecast the number of Fin outcomes and review the limits attached to the selected plan. Check the official page before buying.</p>
<p><strong>Budget note:</strong> outcome-based AI charges can change the monthly bill as customer volume changes. A team that mainly needs email ticketing may not need the extra messaging layer.</p>


<h2 id="hubspot">5. HubSpot Service Hub: for CRM-first support</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that want service conversations connected to HubSpot customer records, sales context and the wider customer platform. HubSpot's official product catalog lists Service Hub Starter from US$20 per Core Seat/month, Professional from US$100 per Service Seat/month and Enterprise from US$150 per Service Seat/month, with annual billing stated for Enterprise. HubSpot also includes select service features in its free tools.</p>
<p>This route is useful when agents need the same customer history that sales and account teams use. The cost comparison should include the seat type, the other HubSpot products the business already pays for, and any required onboarding or credits. Do not compare a Core Seat with a Service Seat as if they provide identical access.</p>
<p><strong>Seat types matter:</strong> a CRM platform can be more than a help desk team needs. Costs and administration can grow if you add more hubs or paid seats just to solve a support problem.</p>


<h2 id="front">6. Front: for shared inbox collaboration and omnichannel support</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that manage customer communication across shared inboxes and need clear collaboration between people. Front's official pricing page lists Starter at US$25 per seat/month for up to 10 seats, Professional at US$65 for up to 50 seats, and Enterprise at US$105 per seat/month. The page showed annual billing and a 24% saving compared with monthly billing.</p>
<p>Starter focuses on shared inbox, ticketing, basic analytics, a public knowledge base and up to ten automation rules. Professional adds omnichannel support and more automation and reporting. Enterprise includes custom roles and permissions, multi-language knowledge content and several AI features. Confirm the current plan table and any AI add-on before estimating total cost.</p>
<p><strong>Seat cap to check:</strong> seat limits and add-on pricing matter. Front can cost more than the base seat price if your team needs AI features on a lower plan.</p>


<h2 id="gorgias">7. Gorgias: for ecommerce support</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> ecommerce teams that need support conversations connected to store activity. Gorgias lists integrations for Shopify, BigCommerce, Magento and WooCommerce and describes its help desk as ticket-volume priced rather than per agent. Its official pricing page describes plans spanning 50 to 5,000 tickets per month and an AI Agent that is charged when it resolves a conversation. These details were checked against the official page.</p>
<p>That model can work when order-related questions are the core of support and ticket volume is a better cost driver than headcount. Forecast busy months as well as quiet months. The official page's price details were not available in the rendered text we checked, so use its live calculator instead of relying on a number copied from an older article.</p>
<p><strong>Volume risk:</strong> a ticket-volume model can make costs less predictable when campaigns or seasonal peaks increase incoming requests. Check what counts as a billable ticket and how AI outcomes are charged before comparing the monthly total.</p>


<h2 id="liveagent">8. LiveAgent: for teams that need multiple support channels</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> teams that want email ticketing, live chat, call centre tools, social channels and a knowledge base in one support product. LiveAgent's pricing page showed a Starter promotion of US$11 per agent/month with annual billing, limited to five agents, and a US$15 monthly-billing offer. The regular rates displayed were higher. The offer can change.</p>
<p>LiveAgent is worth comparing when your support team needs both digital tickets and voice or live chat. The pricing table also shows different agent caps and email-account limits by tier, so check those limits against the number of addresses and people you actually need.</p>
<p><strong>Renewal check:</strong> promotional pricing is not a safe long-term budget. Compare the regular renewal price and the feature caps before choosing it for a larger team.</p>


<h2 id="migration">How to leave Freshdesk without losing useful records</h2>
<p>Plan the export before you cancel. Freshdesk's <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">ticket export guide</a> describes exporting filtered ticket lists to CSV or Excel. That export is not the same as a full account export and may not contain the full conversation history or archived records you need. Review the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225487-how-do-i-export-my-helpdesk-data-">account data export guide</a> and verify the options in your own account.</p>
<ol>
<li>List the data you must keep: tickets, replies, attachments, users, companies, custom fields, knowledge articles, macros, automations and reports.</li>
<li>Check the destination product's current import documentation. Do not assume every field or ticket metric can be transferred.</li>
<li>Export a small, representative sample and confirm the encoding, timestamps, attachments and requester data.</li>
<li>Map Freshdesk fields to the destination system. Record fields that cannot be mapped and decide how the team will access that history.</li>
<li>Test the import in a non-production workspace when the destination offers one. Compare record counts and inspect a sample of conversations.</li>
<li>Agree on a cutover date, ticket ownership and the plan for requests that arrive during the switch.</li>
<li>Keep an approved, access-controlled copy until the destination owner confirms the records are present, and agree on the cutover time for requests that arrive during the switch.</li>
</ol>
<p>Do not delete the source account until the destination owner signs off. A migration is not complete just because the new inbox accepts tickets.</p>

<h2 id="choose">How to choose the right Freshdesk alternative</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Write down the gap first.</strong> Is the issue price, a missing channel, reporting, routing, AI cost or data export? Pick the product that solves that gap.</li>
<li><strong>Compare equivalent seats and plans.</strong> Match agent count, channels, knowledge tools, SLA rules and reporting before comparing the total.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate usage fees.</strong> Include AI outcomes, sessions, ticket overages, add-ons and extra inboxes where the vendor charges for them.</li>
<li><strong>Check your existing stack.</strong> A CRM or ecommerce integration may matter more than a longer feature list.</li>
<li><strong>Run a sample migration.</strong> Check imports and exports before moving every ticket or cancelling Freshdesk.</li>
<li><strong>Price the renewal, not only the offer.</strong> Promotional rates can change. Record the regular rate, billing cycle, taxes and renewal terms.</li>
</ul>
<p>If Freshdesk already covers the workflow, do not switch only because another product advertises more features. Use the <a href="/freshdesk/review/">Freshdesk review</a> and <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">Freshdesk pricing guide</a> to confirm what you would lose or gain. The <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk comparison</a> is a useful next step if Zendesk is on your shortlist.</p>

<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is the best alternative to Freshdesk?</h3><p>There is no single best choice. Zoho Desk suits teams that value its free edition and Zoho connections, Help Scout suits shared inbox workflows, Zendesk suits teams that need broader support controls, and Gorgias is worth checking for ecommerce.</p>
<h3>Is there a free Freshdesk alternative?</h3><p>Zoho Desk has a Free edition for up to three users, Help Scout's Free plan includes five users, one inbox and one Docs site, and HubSpot offers select service tools free. Check each plan's limits before switching.</p>
<h3>Is Freshdesk cheaper than Zendesk?</h3><p>On annual billing, Freshdesk Growth is US$19 per agent/month and Zendesk Support Team is US$19 per agent/month, checked October 28, 2026. Compare the features in the specific tier; the same starting price does not mean the same scope.</p>
<h3>Can you export tickets from Freshdesk?</h3><p>Freshdesk documents filtered ticket-list exports to CSV or Excel, but that export is not the same as a full account export. Use the account export path where available and test a sample before cancelling.</p>
<h3>Which alternative suits an ecommerce store?</h3><p>Gorgias is built around ecommerce support and connects with platforms such as Shopify. Compare its ticket-volume pricing and AI charges with your actual monthly ticket count.</p>
<h3>What should a team check before leaving Freshdesk?</h3><p>Confirm destination import options, export tickets and attachments, map users and custom fields, test a small import, preserve reports and macros where possible, and schedule cutover after owners approve the sample.</p>

